% vim: ft=tex
\chapter{Kernel CN:\ Static semantics}\label{chap:kernel-statics}

% chktex-file 25, underscore in Ott

\margintoc{}%
%
I have covered a large amount of background to the type system so far:
\intro{Core}, liquid types, bidirectional type systems, linear types, precise
separation logic assertions, monadic syntax for the latter and its relation to
kernel syntax for types, let-normalisation and explicit resource terms in
\kl{ResCore}.

In this chapter, I use all of the ingredients defined in the previous two
(\fancyref{chap:formal-background}, \fancyref{chap:kernel-grammar}) to define the
\kl{Kernel CN} type system. Some of the sections are based on my contributions
to~\textcite{pulte2023cn}.%

\intro{Kernel CN} is a formalism for \kl{CN} which assumes that universal
quantifiers are explicitly instantiated, that all existential quantifiers have
explicit witnesses, and all \kl{resource manipulations} have \kl{linearly}
typed \kl{proof terms} embedded into the program itself. It does not require
proof terms for the logical properties, since by construction all of the
entailments fall into (decidable) SMT fragment. \kl{Kernel CN} is a
\kl{bidirectional} type system for \kl{ResCore}.

The lack of inference makes \kl{Kernel CN} simpler to prove
sound, whilst still demonstrating all the aforementioned ingredients.
Since it handles the majority of C, the entire system is very large
(\fancyref{chap:defns}), and so I will only discuss the main features
(\cref{tab:judgements}).

There are additional minor differences between the implementation and the
formalisation. As I mentioned in \fancyref{sec:desugaring}, the formalisation has a
richer grammar of resources: this makes defining predicates to represent tagged
unions more succinct, and allows for opening predicates in more cases.

Along with the type system, I briefly discuss a formalisation of two different
\kl{elaboration} algorithms: one for inferring quantifier instantiations, one for
inferring indices for \kl{iterated predicates}. Note that precise inference
scheme used by \kl{CN}'s implementation is frequently
changing,\sidenote{See
    \href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/commit/7c2c0a364a4373e4eb109f32d01cc9584f51e81f}{Ceberus 7c2c0a36},
    \href{https://github.com/rems-project/cn/commit/d9f0d4895c889af0cb680ca849715fca18b9b946}{CN d9f0d489}.}\label{sn:new-inf-statics}%
so the rules serve to highlight the core principles involved.

