\section{Contexts}

The contexts for the static semantics consist of four parts: (1) \sm{[[ C ]]}
containing the \intro{computational variables} from the \kl{Core} program; (2) \sm{[[ L ]]}
containing purely \intro{logical variables} mentioned in \kl(CN){specifications}; (3) \sm{[[ N ]]},
the \intro{constraint context}, containing a list of (non-quantified) SMT constraints;
and (4) \sm{[[R]]} a \kl{linear context} containing \kl(type){resources}.
I assume a \kl{constraint context} of only non-quantified constraints because users
are required to manually instantiate quantified constraints.

\section{Pure values and expressions}\label{sec:pure-val-expr}

\kl{ResCore} programs have both \kl{computational} and logical (ghost) terms,
which have \kl{base types} \sm{[[ base_type ]]},
which are things like unit, booleans, (mathematical) integers,\sidenote{After the formalisation was completed,
\kl{CN} switched from using mathematical unbounded integers to bit-vectors
(\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/commit/8fdd4198750446de3b44d00f9e8f185db9610fab}{around
commit 8fdd41987}) to better support bit-twiddling idioms, used heavily
in the \kl{buddy allocator} in pKVM, without resorting to many lemmas.}
pointer, records. Each C type \sm{[[ ct ]]} is mapped to a corresponding base type \sm{[[ to_base ( ct ) ]]}
\textemdash{} for example, \sm{[[ to_base ( int * ) ]] = [[ loc ]]}.

\kl{Logical terms} are variously referred to as \sm{[[term]]}, \sm{[[iguard]]} for boolean
index guards of \kl{iterated predicates}, \sm{[[ptr]]} for pointers, \sm{[[iarg]]} for
\kl{input arguments},  \sm{[[oarg]]} for \kl{output arguments}, and later,
\sm{[[alloct]]} for constraints about the allocation history.

As seen in \cref{fig:typing-pval-pexpr}, the rules for \kl{pure
values}\sidenote{\kl{Core} factors out representation of \kl{memory objects}
such as integers, pointers, arrays and structs, as part of the \kl{memory
object model}, so I have followed suit.} are plain synthesis
judgements of the form \sm{[[ C |- pval => base_type ]]};  given \kl{computational
context} and a \kl{pure value}, \kl{synthesise} a \kl{base type}.

Building on top of this (in the same figure), the \kl{pure expression}
\kl{synthesis} judgement is similar \sm{[[ C ; L ; N |- pexpr => pure_ret ]]}.
Given a \kl{computational context}, a \kl{logical context}, a \kl{constraint
context} and a \kl{pure expression}, \kl{synthesise} a \emph{pure} \kl{return type}
(a return type without any \kl(type){resources} or
\kl{logical variables}).

The type \sm{[[pure_ret]] \mathop{{\Colon}{=}} [[ sigma y : base_type .: term /\ I ]]}
is the \kl{Kernel CN} version of the traditional
\sm{\left\{ \, y \in \beta \mid \mathit{ term } \, \right\}} \kl{refinement type}.
Recall that \sm{\Sigma} binds the \kl{computational value} the
\kl{pure values} and \kl(pure){expressions} will evaluate to, and \sm{[[term]]}
constrains that value to be equal to lifted counterparts.
 
