\section{Pure statements}\label{sec:pure-stmt}

\kl{Pure statements} are typed with \kl{checking} judgements
instead of \kl{synthesising} ones, of the form
\sm{[[ C ; L ; N |- pstmt <= pure_ret ]]}.
Given the \kl(ctx){computational}, \kl(ctx){logical} and \kl{constraint
contexts}, check the \kl{pure statement} satisfies this type.

Here, we begin to see \kl{liquid typing}, \kl{let-normalisation} (\cref{sec:rescore}) and the
\kl{bidirectionality} (\cref{sec:bidir-subtyping}) pay off. A \kl{pure statement}
value is checked against a \kl{pure return type} by checking if its constraint
is entailed by the context, using the SMT solver.
Similarly, \coreinline{undef} \kl{checks} against \emph{any} type,
so long as the \kl{constraint context} is inconsistent.
Conditional statements check each branch, assuming the \kl{path condition} or
its negation. Let-statements sequence the evaluation of \kl{pure expressions}.
Typing them requires \kl{synthesising} a type for the bound \kl{pure expression},
assuming a constraint on the \emph{shape} of its resulting value,
and then \kl{checking} the rest of the \kl{pure statement}.

The shape constraint is produced by a judgement which, given a
\kl{computational pattern} (one inherited from Core) and a \kl{base type},
produces a \kl{computational context} and a \sm{[[term]]} corresponding to the
pattern. To simplify matters, I assume all patterns are complete.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnPureXXStmt{}}{%
        \cndefnPureXXStmtXXPVal{},
        \cndefnPureXXStmtXXUB{},
        \cndrulePureXXStmtXXIf{},
        \cndrulePureXXStmtXXLet{}
    }
    \cndefnPatXXComp{}%
    \caption{Selection of \kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for \kl{pure statements},
    including the translation of \kl{computational patterns} into a
    \kl{computational context} used in the \coreinline{let} rule.}\label{fig:typing-pstmt}
\end{figure*}

\section{Resource terms}\label{sec:typing-res-terms}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnResXXSyn{}
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} synthesising typing rules for resource
        terms.}\label{fig:typing-res-term-synth}
\end{figure*}

Typing resource terms requires the addition of an extra \kl{linear context}
\sm{[[ R ]]} of \kl(type){resources}.%
% I will start with the \kl{synthesising}
% judgements first, and move on to checking judgements after.
For readers more familiar with program logics, the following abuse
of notation may provide a helpful intuition.

A judgement \sm{[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- res_term => res ]]} (or similarly for
checking) is the \kl{Kernel CN} version of a proof of
\sm{[[N]] \wedge [[R]] \Rightarrow [[res]]}
using only structural rules as dictated by \sm{[[res_term]]}.

\subsection{Synthesis for resource terms}\label{subsec:synth-res-term}

As visible in \cref{fig:typing-res-term-synth}, unsurprisingly, \sm{\cnkw{emp}}
requires an empty context, and a variable use requires a singleton context.
Using variables also `simplifies' them by partially-evaluating
top-level \sm{\cnkw{if}} when their conditions
or their negations are entailed. \kl{Predicate terms}
\kl{synthesise} their types by checking their arguments are
of the correct type; the same is true for \kl{iterated predicate terms}.
Several features are noteworthy.
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \kl(resource){context} is a singleton because of \kl{linearity}.
    \item The lookup into the \kl(resource){context} is by \kl{predicate name}
        \sm{\alpha} and its \kl{input arguments}, not by variable.
    \item The \kl{output argument} is \kl{synthesised} from the context.
\end{itemize}

For \sm{\cnkw{Owned}} predicates, the input is the pointer, and the
output will be the pointee; for \kl{user-defined predicates}, the input is
their arguments, and output is their return values. I defer explanation of
\sm{\cnkw{Alloc}} and \sm{\cnkw{Padding}} predicates until
\fancyref{chap:cn-vip}. Only \kl{user-defined predicates} can be folded, so
\sm{\cnkw{Owned}}, \sm{\cnkw{Alloc}} and \sm{\cnkw{Padding}}
are excluded (by using \sm{{id}} instead of \sm{\alpha}).

% A term to be folded is simply the (partially-evaluated) body of some predicate,
% so it does not have the information required to know which predicate it could
% belong to, especially because resources can be moved between predicates.

Morally, \sm{\cnkw{fold}} should be checked, because nominally different
predicates can have the same definition.%
% However, in a prior version of the implementation, users were required to
% manually fold and unfold predicates, and (by
% accident) that annotation was placed in an effectful expression. Expressions,
% especially memory actions, are better suited to synthesise types, and so this
% forces the fold/unfold annotations, and the resource terms it contained, to
% also be synthesising.\sidenote{Of course, despite all this, because the
% resource assertions are all assumed to be precise, we could infer the output
% argument, but {Kernel CN} is meant to assume no inference of instantiations
% to logical quantifiers, i.e.\ output arguments of resources.}
However, by historical accident, \sm{\cnkw{fold}} terms are
annotated, so that they can be used in a \kl{synthesising} position.
The \sm{\mathit{res\_term}} it carries \kl{checked} against the \emph{definition}
(`body') of that predicate, with the \kl[iarg]{input} and \kl{output arguments}
substituted in (if they are of the correct type).

\subsection{Synthesis for predicate operations}\label{sec:synth-predops}

\kl{Predicate operations} are also \kl{synthesising} and have their own
judgement. And because these operations can combine more than one kind of
\kl(type){resource}, they need to \kl{synthesise} separating conjunctions,
so the rule for them is also synthesising.

For space, I only present the rules for turning ownership of fixed-length arrays
into an iterated separating conjunctions and back (\cref{fig:typing-predops}).

The first rule says that if \sm{\mathit{res\_term}} synthesises ownership of a
fixed-length array, then the iteration of that synthesises an iterated
separating conjunction for ownership over the indices of that array (\sm{0} to
\sm{n-1}). The second rule does the inverse, but includes some additional checks
to prevent two adjacent locations of the same type of different
allocations, from being indexed by the same pointer (as required by the
\kl{memory object model}).

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnPredOps{}}{%
        \cndrulePredOpsXXIterate{},
        \cndrulePredOpsXXCongeal{}
    }
    \caption{Selection of \kl{Kernel CN} synthesising typing rules for
        predicate operations.}\label{fig:typing-predops}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Checking for resource terms}\label{subsec:checking-res-terms}

Some \kl{resource terms} are \kl{checked}, such as \sm{\cnkw{term}}, which
represents a constraint to be proven by the SMT solver, but does not state
which one. Similarly, existential types are also \kl{checking} because it is
impossible to infer them in the general case.\sidenote{Consider a heap $1 \mapsto 1$, and consider that all
of $\exists x.\ 1 \mapsto 1$, $\exists x.\ x \mapsto 1$, $\exists x.\ 1 \mapsto x$
(imprecise), $\exists x.\ x \mapsto x$ are valid types describing it.}
% The witness \textemdash{} carried by the {\small $\cnkw{pack}$} \textemdash{}
% is substituted into the inner type, which is turn is used to check the inner
% $\mathit{res\_term}$.

As I mentioned earlier (\cref{sec:res-terms}), \emph{any} \kl{resource term} can
introduce an \kl{ordered-disjunction}, and so any \kl{resource term} can be
\kl{checked} by it. Furthermore, at most \emph{one} of the branches may be \kl{checked},
and which one depends on whether the condition or its negation is entailed. I
intentionally avoid the more permissive rule below because that would lead to
the same \kl{resource term} being typed twice, incurring an exponential cost.

{\small%
\[
  \inferrule{ [[ C ; L ; N , term ; R |- res_term <= res1 ]] \\ [[ C ; L ; N , neg term ; R |- res_term <= res2 ]] }
            { [[ C ; L ; N , term ; R |- res_term <= if term then res1 else res2 ]] }
\]}

If neither the condition nor its negation is entailed, I call the condition
(and the resource) \intro{under-determined}. In this case, the system may
attempt to \kl{synthesise} a type for the \kl(resource){term} and check that
the \kl{synthesised} \kl(resource){type} is equal to the
\kl{ordered-disjunction}. Only variables can \kl{synthesise} an
\kl{ordered-disjunction} type, that too only if the condition is
\kl{under-determined} (otherwise simplification will reduce it to either
branch).

\kl{Separating conjunctions} are also checked to allow for the case where an
existential or an \kl{ordered-disjunction} are in the sub-components.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnResXXChk{}}{%
        \cndruleResXXChkXXPhi{},
        \cndruleResXXChkXXPack{},
        %\cndruleResXXChkXXSepConj{},
        \cndruleResXXChkXXIfXXTrue{},
        \cndruleResXXChkXXIfXXFalse{},
        %\cndruleResXXChkXXSwitch{}
    }
    \caption{Selection of \kl{Kernel CN} checking typing rules for
        resource terms.}\label{fig:typing-res-term-check}
\end{figure*}

At any point, if the \kl(resource){term} cannot be \kl{checked} by any other
rule, the system can switch to \kl{synthesising} a type and then comparing
whether the two \kl(resource){types} are structurally equal, modulo SMT
entailment (\cref{fig:typing-res-term-check}).

\begin{marginfigure}
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndruleResXXChkXXSwitch{}
    \caption{Switching from checking to synthesising types for resource
        terms.}\label{fig:typing-res-term-switch}
\end{marginfigure}

\section{Memory actions and pointer operations}\label{sec:kernel-mem-action-ops}

As with \kl{resource terms}, \kl{memory actions} require all the contexts
(\kl(ctx){computational}, \kl(ctx){logical}, \kl(ctx){constraint} and
\kl(ctx){resource}), using the following judgement:
\[
[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- res_action => ret ]].
\]
Given the contexts, a (\kl{resource term} annotated) \kl{memory action}
\kl{synthesises} a return type.

For readers familiar with program logics, the following abuse of notation
may provide a helpful intuition.

A judgement \sm{[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- res_term | action => sigma y : base_type .: ret ]]}
is the \kl{Kernel CN} version of a proof of
\sm{\{\, [[N]] \wedge [[R]] \,\} \, [[action]] \, \{\, y {:} [[base_type]].\ [[ret]]\, \}},
where \sm{[[res_term]]} dictates the required structural rules.

The rule for \sm{\cnkw{create}} requires no resources from the context
\sm{[[cdot]]}, and thus only \sm{[[emp]]} as its resource term.
Its return type speaks of \sm{y_p}, the pointer to the newly created allocation,
which is constrained by \sm{[[term]]} as required by the C standard (omitted for brevity).
It also mentions the unconstrained \kl{logical value} \sm{x}, the pointee of the new
allocation, and the two resources it creates. The first is \kl{write-only
ownership} the new allocation, and the second is an \kl{allocation token}
\sm{\cnkw{Alloc}}, which is explain in \cref{chap:cn-vip}.

Loads consume a \kl{read-write permission}\sidenote{For further
flexibility, loads of structs and unions could be partially or completely
uninitialised, as allowed by the C standard, see
note~\ref{sn:partial-init-read}.} (premise 2), check if the address of the load
and the address of the \kl{permission} are symbolically equal (premise 3), and
\kl{synthesise} a \kl{return type} mentioning the loaded value and an identical
\kl{read-write permission}. Stores are similar, but with extra checks on
the stored value (premise 3). Note that the stored value is placed in the
return type. Lastly, the rule for \sm{\cnkw{kill}} is the converse of the
one for \sm{\cnkw{create}}: it \emph{consumes} the \kl{ownership} and
\kl{allocation token} for a location (premise 2), checks that the addresses and
allocations being considered are the same (premises 3 and 4) and
\kl{synthesises} a \kl{return type} with no resources.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \begingroup%
    \NewCommandCopy{\origcndrule}{\cndrule}
    \renewcommand{\cndrule}[4][]{\origcndrule[#1]{#2}{%
    \begin{minipage}{0.65\textwidth}
        \raggedright%
        \leftskip2em
        \parindent-2em
        $#3$
    \end{minipage}
    }{#4}}%
    \NewCommandCopy{\origXXcndruleActionXXCreate}{\cndruleActionXXCreate}
    \renewcommand{\cndruleActionXXCreate}{\onlyUseNthCnPremise{1,3}{\origXXcndruleActionXXCreate{}}}
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnAction{}}{%
        \cndruleActionXXCreate{},
        \cndruleActionXXLoad{},
        \cndruleActionXXStore{},
        \cndruleActionXXKillXXStatic{}
    }
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMemop{}}{%
        \cndruleMemopXXRelXXBinop{}
    }
    \endgroup%
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for \kl{memory actions}, and a select
        rule for typing a \kl{memory operation}. I will explain the roles of
        $[[Alloc]]$ in \ActionCreate{} and \ActionKillStatic{}, and the
        liveness and bounds checks used in \MemopRelBinop{}
        in~\fancyref{chap:cn-vip}.}\label{fig:typing-mem-action}
\end{figure*}

Rules for typing \kl{memory operations}, \ie{} `effectful' pointer operations
require the pointers to be in bounds of a live allocation, and \kl{synthesise}
a \kl{return type}. There are many of them, but they
share the same principles so it suffices to only explain one
(\cref{fig:typing-mem-action}). Using a binary relational operator (not
equality) is only valid if both pointers are live, belong to the same
allocation and are within the bounds of the same. The \kl{return type}
\kl{synthesises} the result of that relational operator applied to the
addresses of those pointers, plus any resources consumed to prove the pointer
is live.

\section{Spine judgement}\label{sec:spine-judgement}

\intro{Spines (lists of values)} are used in calls to pure Core functions,
elaborated C functions, effectful Core procedures, and the \sm{\cnkw{run}}
operator. Unusually, they are also used as
\kl[spine]{values} for statements, written \sm{[[ < ret_terms > ]]}.

The process for checking all the uses is the same; the straightforward
specification of these rules (\cref{fig:typing-spine}) is the pay-off for
desugaring (\cref{sec:desugaring}) the \kl{CN} types into \kl{kernel types}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnSpine{}
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for function
        spines.}\label{fig:typing-spine}
\end{figure*}

The judgement for typing \kl{spines} is \sm{[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- param_spine :: fon >> subs ; ret ]]},
which means that given the contexts, a list of parameter-argument pairs and a
\kl{function type}, produce a substitution \sm{[[ subs ]]} and a \kl{return type} \sm{[[ ret ]]}.

\kl{Function types} depend on \kl(var){computational} and \kl{logical (ghost)
variables}; for notational convenience, I assume that these are the same as the
parameters in the spine. Checking \kl[pval]{computational values} and
\kl{logical terms} requires substituting them into the rest of the type,
and checking the rest of the \kl{spine}. The resulting substitution is then extended
with the same.\sidenote{Building the substitution this way, rather than
`tail-recursively', made it easier to prove properties about it by induction
(\cref{sec:sub-ctxts}).}\label{sn:tail-rec-sub} Constraints in the type are
checked with the SMT solver. \kl{Resource terms} are checked with only part of
the \kl{resource context}; the rest of the \kl{spine} is checked with the remainder.
\kl{Function types} do not dependent on \kl{resource variables}, but function
bodies do, so substitutions must also be extended with \kl{resource terms}.

\section{Effectful values and expressions}\label{sec:eff-val-expr}

\kl{Spine} typing is used for typing \sm{\cnkw{ccall}} and \sm{\cnkw{pcall}}
(effectful) Core expressions (\cref{fig:typing-ret-terms}). The
judgement for this is \sm{[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- expr => ret ]]}, which says that
given the contexts and an \kl{effectful expression}, \kl{synthesise} a
\kl{return type}. Both lookup a \kl{function type} for the
callee,\sidenote{\kl{CN} only supports indirect function calls when the callees
are explicitly enumerated.} and \kl{synthesise} the \kl{return type}
% They are identical to typing for $[[< ret_terms > ]]$, with the main
% difference that (a) the judgement $[[ C ; L ; N ; R |- < ret_terms > <= ret ]]$
% is checking and (b) the return type must be mapped to its dual function type
% before using the spine judgement.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnExpr{}}{%
        \cndruleExprXXCCall{},
        \cndruleExprXXProc{}
    }
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnStmt{}}{
        \cndruleStmtXXRetT{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for effectful expressions and
        return-terms.}\label{fig:typing-ret-terms}
\end{figure*}

\section{pattern-matching}\label{sec:pat-match}

\intro{Pattern-matching} is an unusually important for \kl{Kernel CN} because
it is \emph{the funnel} through which \kl(type){resource} and \kl{return
types} enter contexts.

The judgement for this is \sm{[[ C ; L ; N |- ret_pat : ret ~> C' ; L' ; N' ; R' ]]}.
This says that given the contexts, a \kl{return pattern} and a \kl{return
type}, \kl{synthesise} \kl(ctx){computational}, \kl(ctx){logical},
\kl(ctx){constraint} and \kl{resource contexts}.

\kl{Pattern-matching} for \kl{return types} (\cref{fig:typing-ret-pat}) is an
extension of pattern-matching for \kl{Core} values (\cref{fig:typing-pstmt}),
complicated by the fact that \kl{return types} are dependent on
\kl(var){computational} and \kl{logical (ghost) variables}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnPatXXRet{}
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for pattern-matching at return
        types.}\label{fig:typing-ret-pat}
\end{figure*}

\PatRetComp{} \kl{synthesises} a \kl{computational context} \sm{[[ C1 ]]} and
a representative \sm{[[ term1 ]]} of the expected shape. The former extends both
the input and output context; the latter is substituted into the rest of the
\kl{return type} for the recursive call.\sidenote{Similar to substitutions (see
note~\ref{sn:tail-rec-sub}), I build the contexts this way, rather than
`tail-recursively', to make it easier to prove properties about it by induction
(\cref{sec:sub-ctxts}).}


\PatRetLog{} extends the \kl{logical context} with the pattern
variable,\sidenote{\kl{CN} added support for algebraic datatypes after I
    formalised \kl{Kernel CN}
    (\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/commit/0e5020732df2ec444ffa7a67abcc7c2c82904d24}{commit
0e50207}) and so and I did not formalise logical pattern-matching, which would
be similar to the computational case.}, and is otherwise similar to
\PatRetComp{}.

For any pattern, \PatRetPhi{} adds the constraint to the contexts
\kl{synthesised} by the rest of the \kl{return type}. Lastly, \PatRetRes{} is
similar to \PatRetComp{}, but \kl{synthesises} and extends
\kl(ctx){logical}, \kl(ctx){constraint} and \kl{resource contexts}.

\kl{Resource types} are destructed against \kl{resource patterns} to
\kl{synthesise} contexts (\cref{fig:typing-res-pat}). The judgement for this is
\sm{[[ L ; N |- res_pat : res ~> L' ; N' ; R' ]]}, which means that given
contexts,\sidenote{I abuse the notation slightly and omit the computational
context \sm{[[ C ]]} because it remains the same in all the rules.} a resource
pattern and a \kl(resource){type}, \kl{synthesise} \kl(ctx){logical},
\kl(ctx){constraint} and \kl{resource contexts}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnPatXXResXXMatch{}
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for pattern-matching at resource
        types.}\label{fig:typing-res-pat}
\end{figure*}

I will present only the rules for destructing \kl{ordered-disjunctions} and
fold-patterns; the rest are as expected. For the former, dual to its checking
rule, its condition or its negation must be entailed, after which the
corresponding branch is destructed against the same pattern. In the case of
\kl{under-determined} conditions, only the wildcard or variable pattern will
match. For the latter, the pattern only destructs when the type is a
\kl{predicate} and the nested pattern destructs against the definition of
that predicate (with the arguments substituted).

\section{Effectful statements}\label{sec:eff-stmt}

With \kl{pattern-matching} in hand, I can explain the typing rules for \kl{effectful
statements} (\cref{fig:typing-stmt}). I will focus only on \sm{\cnkw{let}}
and \sm{\cnkw{run}} statements, the rest are
straightforward and in~\fancyref{sec:explicit-system}.

\kl{Effectful statements} are \kl{checked}, but the bound
\kl(effectful){expression} in the \sm{\cnkw{let}} is \kl{synthesised}, so
that the \kl(return){type} can be used for \kl{pattern-matching}. The
\kl{synthesised} contexts from that are then appended to the existing ones to
\kl{check} the body of the \sm{\cnkw{let}}.

For typing \sm{\cnkw{run}}, the rule looks up the \kl{function type}
belonging to the label, and then \kl{checks} that the \kl{spine} satisfies it.
Unlike \sm{\cnkw{ccall}} or \sm{\cnkw{pcall}}, control flow does
not return from \sm{\cnkw{run}}, and so the \kl{synthesised}
\kl(return){type} is expected to be false, and it \kl{checks} against any
\kl(return){type}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnStmt{}}{%
        \cndruleStmtXXLet{},
        \cndruleStmtXXRun{}
    }
    \caption{Select \kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for effectful statements.}\label{fig:typing-stmt}
\end{figure*}

\section{Elaboration}\label{sec:elaboration}

I have also formalised an elaboration system as part of \kl{Kernel CN}. When I
wrote it, \kl{CN} required users to manually fold and unfold all
\kl(user-defined){predicates}, but inferred indices for \kl{iterated
predicates} automatically. Now, \kl{CN} automatically folds and unfolds
\kl{non-recursive predicates} with a single clause, and requires users to state
which indices can be moved in and out of any \kl{iterated predicates} in the
context.\sidenote{See
\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/commit/7c2c0a364a4373e4eb109f32d01cc9584f51e81f}{Ceberus
7c2c0a36}.} Instantiations for logical quantifiers continue to be inferred.

The goal of elaboration is to take a well-formed \kl{ResCore} program, without
\kl{explicit resources terms} or quantifier instantiations, and return the same
program with those pieces filled in, such that it would pass the aforementioned
typing rules (if it succeeds). I have no proof of this, so I do not claim the
following rules meet this goal. Instead, I present it to clarify the principles
behind \kl{CN}'s implementation.

\begin{table*}
\small%
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.85\textwidth}cc}
\toprule
Name & Judgement & Figure (Section) \\
\midrule
\textsc{Oarg*}
    & $[[ y ; N ; nR |- res ~> bind res_bind for res_term and oarg -| nR' ]]$
    &~\ref{fig:elab-res-oarg}
\\
\textsc{Req*}
   & $[[ N ; nR |- pred_ipred ~> bind res_bind for res_term and oarg -| nR' ]]$
   &~(\ref{subsec:app-synth-indices})
\\
\textsc{If*}
   & $[[ N ; nR |- if term then res1 else res2 ~> r -| nR' ]]$
   &~(\ref{subsec:app-synth-oargs})
\\
\textsc{Elab\_Expr*}
   & $[[ C ; L ; N ; nR |- expr ~> bind res_bind for expr' : ret -| nR' ]]$
   &~\ref{fig:elab-fold}
\\
\textsc{Elab\_Action*}
   & $[[ C ; L ; N ; nR |- action ~> bind res_bind for res_term & norm_ret -| nR' ]]$
   &~\ref{fig:elab-action}
\\
\textsc{Elab\_Spine*}
   & $[[ C ; L ; N ; nR |- spine :: norm_fun ~> bind res_bind for spine' and norm_ret -| nR' ]]$
   &~\ref{fig:elab-spine}
\\
\textsc{Res\_Diff*}
   & $[[ N |- r : norm_res -? pred_ipred ~> res_diff ]]$
   &~\ref{fig:res-diff-def}
\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Summary of elaboration judgements presented and their sections; the
    full list of judgements can be found in~\cref{sec:metavars-grammar}
    and all their rules can be found in~\cref{sec:elab-system}.}\label{tab:elab-judgements}
\end{table*}

\cref{tab:elab-judgements} presents only a slice of the elaboration to
highlight the key judgements; the full system is in~\fancyref{sec:elab-system}.

\textsc{Oarg*} reads as follows: given an output parameter \sm{[[y]]},
\kl{constraint context} \sm{[[N]]}, a \kl{resource context} \sm{[[nR]]}, and a
\kl{resource type} \sm{[[res]]}, \kl{synthesise} a series of bindings
\sm{[[res_bind]]} for a \kl{resource term} \sm{[[res_term]]} and \kl{output argument}
\sm{[[oarg]]} which will result in the updated \kl{resource context} \sm{[[nR']]}.
The variable \sm{[[y]]} represents the output of the \kl(type){resource}, it
is threaded through only for the \OargIfUndet{} rule
(\cref{fig:elab-res-oarg}), when we learn nothing new about the \kl{output
argument} and so just return it instead.

\textsc{Oarg*} \kl{synthesises} a \kl{checking} \kl{resource term}, however,
the main place this judgement is used (by historical accident) is in a
\kl{synthesis} position (\cref{fig:elab-fold}). As mentioned earlier
(\cref{subsec:synth-res-term}), this is why \sm{\cnkw{fold}} is an
annotated \kl{synthesising} \kl(resource){term}, when morally it is a
\kl{checking} \kl(resource){term}.

\textsc{Oarg*} is used in \ElabExprFold, in~\cref{fig:elab-fold}. The
\textsc{Elab\_Expr*} judgement is similar to the \textsc{Oarg*} judgement, but
\kl{synthesising} a \kl{return type} instead of a \kl{resource type} and
\kl{output argument}, and an elaborated \kl{ResCore} expression containing the
\sm{\cnkw{fold}} \kl{resource term}.

\begin{figure}[htp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnElabXXExpr{}}{%
        \cndruleElabXXExprXXFold{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} elaboration rule for manually folding
        predicates.}\label{fig:elab-fold}
\end{figure}

\textsc{Req*} reads as follows: given a \kl{constraint context} \sm{[[N]]},
a \kl(normalised){resource context} \sm{[[nR]]}, and a \kl{resource request}
\sm{[[ pred_ipred ]]}, \kl{synthesise} a series of bindings \sm{[[res_bind]]} for a
\kl{resource term} \sm{[[res_term]]} and \kl{output argument} \sm{[[oarg]]} which
will result in the updated \kl{resource context} \sm{[[nR']]}.

\textsc{Req*}'s rules (\cref{subsec:app-synth-indices}) encode a linear search
of the \kl{resource context}, \kl{synthesising} \kl{predicate operations}
(\cref{sec:synth-predops}) on \kl{(iterated)} \kl{predicates} in the context to
construct the requested \sm{[[pred_ipred]]}.

\textsc{Req*} is sufficient for all other placements of \kl{resource terms}
(\kl{memory actions}, \kl{memory operations}, and \kl{spines}). I show the rule
for \sm{\cnkw{load}}s in \cref{fig:elab-action}, and the same for
\kl{spines} in \cref{fig:elab-spine}. In these cases we see that the output
argument is used only in return types\sidenote{The \kl{synthesised} \kl{return
types} are used in turn to \kl{synthesise} patterns to maintain the
invariant that the \kl{resource context} is always \kl{normalised}.} and
that the \kl{resource terms} and bindings are the important additions into
the program.

\begin{figure}[htp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnElabXXAction{}}{%
        \cndruleElabXXActionXXLoad{}
    }
    \caption{Select \kl{Kernel CN} elaboration rule for \kl{memory
        actions}.}\label{fig:elab-action}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[htp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnElabXXSpine{}}{%
        \cndruleElabXXSpineXXTake{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} elaboration rule for instantiating a logical
        quantifier when elaborating a function call \kl{spine}.}\label{fig:elab-spine}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Normalised resource contexts}\label{sec:norm-ctxt-res}

To specify \kl{elaboration}, I define and use \intro{normalised} \kl{resource
contexts}, signified with an underlined \sm{[[ nR ]]}, containing only
\kl{normalised} \kl{resource types}. A \kl{normalised resource} is either an
\kl{(iterated)} \kl{predicate}, or an \kl{under-determined
resource}. I call it \kl{normalised} because (with the appropriate terms and
pattern-matching), any \kl{resource context} can be manipulated into such a
\kl{normal form}. This means it is simple to specify lookups in the context: if
I need \sm{b \ast c} but the context has \sm{\{\; x {:} (a \ast b) , y {:} (c
\ast d) \}} then how should I proceed? Do I lookup \sm{b} and \sm{c} individually?
Together? Swapped? How do I handle the case where one is a sub-\kl(type){resource} of
another in the context? I avoid these questions and simply require that all
rules which use \kl(type){resource} lookup preserve normality as an invariant. This
requires binding existing terms (\cref{fig:res-bind-def}) to manipulate the
context, carefully retaining `leftover' resources from a lookup.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \small
    \grammartabularSTY{%
        \cnresXXbind{}\cnafterlastrule{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} definition of resource bindings. Note that these are
        only required in the presence of explicit resource terms, and as such
        are not necessary in \kl{CN}'s implementation.}\label{fig:res-bind-def}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Synthesising output arguments}\label{subsec:synth-oargs}

The \textsc{Oarg*} judgement (\cref{fig:elab-res-oarg}) for \kl{synthesising}
\kl{resource terms} and \kl{output arguments} relies on the fact that the more
flexible \kl{Kernel CN} types (\cref{fig:kernel-res}) have come from a
translation (\cref{fig:pred-to-kernel}) of the intentionally rigid surface
syntax (\cref{sec:monadic-syntax}).

% Synthesising a term for $\mathsf{emp}$ is simple and does not change the
% context, and the synthesised output argument is just a dummy value.

\kl{Synthesising} an \kl{output argument} from an equality constraint
(\OargReturn) is just taking the right-hand-side of the equality.

\kl{Synthesising} an \kl{output argument} from a \kl{predicate} (\OargPred)
involves using the \textsc{Req*} judgement to do a linear search of the context
to find an exact match and obtain an \kl{output argument}.

\kl{Synthesising} an \kl{output argument} from an \kl{under-determined
resource} (\OargIfUndet), is similar. It also involves is using a similar
judgement (\textsc{If*}) to do a linear search of the context to find an exact
match, but no \kl{output argument}, returning the output parameter \sm{[[ y ]]}
instead.

\kl{Synthesising} an \kl{output argument} for an existential \sm{[[ exists y1 :
base_type .: res1 * res2 ]]} (\OargTake) is more involved. We have to first
instantiate the existential, which is possible because (by construction) every
existential quantifies over the output argument of (iterated) predicates.
Recursing on \sm{[[ res1 ]]}, we get the required bindings \sm{[[ res_bind1 ]]},
\kl{resource terms} \sm{[[ res_term1 ]]}, \kl{output argument} \sm{[[ oarg1 ]]} and
updated \kl{resource context} \sm{[[ nR1 ]]}. Then we substitute the \kl{output
argument} into \sm{[[ res2 ]]} and then recurse on that to obtain the same (\sm{[[
res_bind2 ]]}, \sm{[[ res_term2 ]]}, \sm{[[ oarg2 ]]}, \sm{[[ nR2 ]]}) for it. The
result is a concatenation of the two bindings, a \sm{\cnkw{pack}} of the
two \kl{resource terms}, the second output argument and the second updated
\kl{resource context}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnOarg{}}{%
        \cndruleOargXXReturn{},
        \cndruleOargXXPred{},
        \cndruleOargXXTake{},
        %\cndruleOargXXIfXXTrue{},
        \cndruleOargXXIfXXUndet{}
    }
    \caption{Select \kl{Kernel CN} rules for an elaboration pass which given
        contexts and a resource type, infers \kl{proof terms} and logical
        quantifier instantiations satisfying those types.}\label{fig:elab-res-oarg}
\end{figure*}


\subsection{Synthesising indices for iterated predicates}\label{subsec:synth-indices}

Inferring indices was possible, and relatively straightforward, because of the
requirement that every predicate had as its first input argument a pointer \sm{p},
and that every \kl{iteration} over index \sm{[[i]]} of such predicates had as its first
argument \sm{[[ array_shift < ct > ( ptrt1 , i ) ]]}. Given this, inferring an
index (for either \sm{\cnkw{break}}ing or \sm{\cnkw{glue}}ing) reduces to
\sm{[[ i = ( ptrt2 - ptrt1 ) / sizeof ct ]]} where \sm{[[ ptr2 ]]} is the pointer in
the first argument of the requested predicate. This is made precise in
\cref{fig:res-diff-break}.

The judgement \sm{[[ N |- r : norm_res -? pred_ipred ~> res_diff ]]} says that
given a \kl{constraint context}, a \kl{normalised} resource, and a request for
a \kl{predicate} \kl{(iterated)}, \kl{synthesise} the `difference' between the
two. As shown in \cref{fig:res-diff-def}, the `difference' can be of four
sorts: no difference, exact match, subset match, overlapping match; each
requires slightly different handling (continue search, stop search, bind
remainder and stop search, bind remainder and continue search respectively)
from the lookup judgement (\cref{subsec:app-synth-indices}).

\begin{figure*}
    \small
    \begingroup%
    \renewcommand{\grammartabularSTY}[1]{\grammartabularWrapComment{0.7\textwidth}{#1}}
    \renewcommand{\outpol}[1]{#1}
    \grammartabularSTY{%
        \cnresXXdiff{}\cnafterlastrule{}
    }
    \endgroup%
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} definition of a `resource difference'.}\label{fig:res-diff-def}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnResXXDiff{}}{%
        \cndruleResXXDiffXXPIXXRem{}
    }
    \caption{Select \kl{Kernel CN} elaboration rule for inferring indices and
        \kl{synthesising} \kl{predicate operations}.}\label{fig:res-diff-break}
\end{figure*}

\chapter{Kernel CN:\ Proof of soundness}%
\label{chap:kernel-soundness}

In this chapter, I discuss the key definitions, lemmas and proof sketches (the
whole proof is in~\cref{chap:soundness}) for establishing the soundness of
\kl{Kernel CN}. I first define an operational semantics for \kl{ResCore}, and
then sketch out a straightforward, syntactic proof of type safety.\sidenote{The
continuation-like behaviour of the \sm{\cnkw{run}} and \sm{\cnkw{save}} operators
make it more challenging to prove soundness denotationally.} This suffices
because \kl{ResCore} is a first-order language, and all functions and labels
are annotated with the correct type, despite the presence of \kl{linear} types,
which usually require logical relation methods.

The proof is split up into three smaller proofs: a joint
progress-and-type-preservation proof for \kl{resource term reduction}, a
progress theorem, and a type-preservation theorem.

The definitions and lemmas of note in this proof are (a) the careful definition
of \kl{substitution}, and its accompanying substitution lemma, (b) the novel
structure of the \kl{heap}, which is unusual in that is essentially just
separation logic predicate and, (c) the interaction between the \kl{resource
terms} and \kl{heaps}.

\section{Substitution and contexts}\label{sec:sub-ctxts}

I start with a \kl{substitution lemma}, which  requires assigning `context
types' to substitutions. The first three rules for typing substitutions
(\cref{fig:soundness-sub}) are straightforward: the empty substitution
represents the empty context, a computational or logical substitution requires
the substituted terms to be well-typed.

A resource substitution also requires its \kl(resource){term} to be well-typed.
However, because \kl{resource terms} and \kl(resource){types} depend on
\kl(ctx){computational} and \kl{logical variables}, scoping needs to be
handled carefully. \kl{Resource types} in a substitution may only mention
variables either in the contexts on the left of the turnstile, or the
\kl(ctx){computational} and \kl{logical context} in the substitution
type.

This scoping discipline makes it easier to decompose substitutions into valid
parts, each of which must be applied sequentially. Hence, the
\textsc{Sub\_Chk\_Concat} rule \kl{checks} not only the first half \sm{\psi} of
the substitution against half the context, but checks the second half
\emph{substituted} \sm{\psi(\sigma)} against second half of the context
\emph{substituted}, to eliminate any mention of variables from \sm{[[ C1 ]]} and
\sm{[[ L1 ]]} in \sm{[[ R'2 ]]}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnSubsXXChk{}
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} substitution typing}\label{fig:soundness-sub}
\end{figure*}

In~\cref{sec:sub}, I use this definition to prove lemmas such as the fact that
substitution preserves SMT results and that substitutions can be split up; the
latter is complicated by the presence of \kl{linearity}. The key one is the
\intro{substitution lemma} stated below.

\begin{lemma}[Substitution lemma]
If \sm{[[ C' ; L' ; N ; R' ]] [[|-]] J}, then
{\small%
\begin{align*}
    [[forall]]\ [[ C ]], [[ L ]], &[[ R ]], [[ subs ]].\ \allowbreak
    \left( [[ C ; L ; subs ( N ) ; R |- subs <= ( C' ; L' ; R' ) ]] \right) [[=>]] \\
    &[[ C ; L ; subs ( N ) ; R ]] [[|-]] [[ subs ]] ( J ).
\end{align*}}

Remember, only \kl{logical terms} \sm{[[term]]}, \kl{resource types} \sm{[[res]]} and
\kl{return types} \sm{[[ret]]} depend on \kl(ctx){computational} or \kl{logical
variables}; base types \sm{[[base_type]]} are simple.

Since \sm{[[ N ]]} is scoped to \sm{[[ C' ]] ; [[ L' ]]}, we must substitute over it
as well as all the usual suspects on the right. This is defined by substituting
over each constraint in \sm{[[ N ]]}. As a result, \sm{[[ subs ( N1 , N2) ]] =
[[ subs ( N1 ) , subs ( N2 ) ]]}, and if \sm{[[ subs ( N ) ]] = [[ N1' , N2' ]]} then
\sm{[[ exists ]] [[ N1 ]], [[ N2 ]] .\: [[ subs ( N1 , N2 ) ]] =
[[ subs ( N1 ) , subs ( N2 ) ]]}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
See~\cref{subsec:sub}, which proceeds by an induction over the typing
judgements, in four parts.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Variable rules.} Repeatedly invert the substitution typing
    assumption until the variable used in the context appears, and then
    use the equational properties of substitution to prove the goal.
\item \StmtRetT. \sm{[[ to_fun ret ]]} commutes with substitution, by leaving
    \sm{[[terms]]} unchanged, so induction proceeds straightforwardly.
\item \textbf{Context change rules.} Split up substitutions as required by the
    restrictions on the resource context. If a rule uses the SMT judgement, use
    the substitution preserves SMT results lemma.
\item \textbf{Remaining rules}. Straightforward induction.
\end{itemize}%
\end{proof}

\section{Heaps and their types}\label{sec:heap-types}

\intro{Heaps} in the operational semantics are, slightly unusually, tree-shaped
collections of \kl{predicates} (\cref{fig:kernel-heap-def}). The leaves of the
tree are the \kl{built-in predicates}; the branches of the \kl{heap} are
\kl{user-defined ones} tagged with their `run-time' definitions (resource
values) and a sub-heap (of the resources used by the definition).

\begin{figure*}
    \small%
    \grammartabularSTY{%
        \cnresXXval{}\cnafterlastrule{}
    }%
    \cngrammarcompressed{\textwidth}{%
        \cnheap{}\cninterrule{}
        \cndefXXheap{}\cninterrule{}
        \cnarrXXheap{}\cnafterlastrule{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{ResCore} dynamics heap definition.}\label{fig:kernel-heap-def}
\end{figure*}

\intro{Resource values} are the subset of \kl{resource terms} which cannot
reduce further. They help capture the idea that \kl{predicates} encapsulate
their contents until unfolded, via \kl(resource){pattern-matching}
(\cref{fig:kernel-unfold}), which flattens the tree-shaped heap by one level.
The nested definition is used to destruct the rest of the
\kl(resource){pattern} to produce a new \kl{heap} and \kl{substitution}.

\begin{figure}[htp]
    \small%
    \ContinuedFloat%
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnSubsXXPatXXRes{}}{%
        \cndruleSubsXXPatXXResXXFold{}
    }\caption{\kl{ResCore} dynamics for unfolding predicates. Note that this
    flattens the structure of the tree-shaped heap by one level.}\label{fig:kernel-unfold}
\end{figure}

In the \sm{\cnkw{fold}} rule (\cref{fig:kernel-fold}), the inverse
happens, which requires splitting a heap into the \kl{footprint} of some
\kl{resource values} (\cref{fig:kernel-footprint}), and the remainder. The
\intro{footprint} of a resource value is the sub-heap containing the (iterated)
predicates it refers to.

\begin{figure}[htp]
    \ContinuedFloat%
    \small%
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnOpXXResT{}}{%
        \cndruleOpXXResTXXFold{}
    }\caption{\kl{ResCore} dynamics for folding predicates. Note the
        sub-heap associated with the predicate, its \emph{footprint},
        is now nested one level deeper within the tree-shaped
        heap.}\label{fig:kernel-fold}
\end{figure}

I defined both the \kl(resource){pattern-matching} and the \kl{resource
term dynamic semantics} in a big-step style; this makes their definitions more
concise, at the cost of intertwining the usually separate proofs of progress
and type preservations.

\begin{figure}[htp]
    \ContinuedFloat%
    \small%
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnOpXXFootprint{}}{%
        \cndruleOpXXFootprintXXPred{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{ResCore} dynamics for calculating the footprint of a
        $[[pred_term]]$ resource value (one which remains folded).
        At runtime, resource values represent the definition of a
        predicate.}\label{fig:kernel-footprint}
\end{figure}

I finish the proof preliminaries by defining the type of a heap: a
\kl{normalised resource context} (\cref{sec:norm-ctxt-res}); the rules for
these are straightforward (\cref{fig:kernel-heap-typing}), except for
\HeapPredOther{}. This rule (implicitly) existentially quantifies over a
\kl(normalised){resource context} \sm{[[nR]]} for which the resource value \sm{[[ def ]]}
and \sm{[[ heap ]]} are well-typed. I use this to prove prove progress for
\kl{ResCore} pattern-matching.

\begin{figure*}
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnHeap{}}{%
        \cndruleHeapXXEmpty{},
        \cndruleHeapXXPredXXOwned{},
        \cndruleHeapXXPredXXOther{}
    }
    \caption{Select typing rules for heaps.}\label{fig:kernel-heap-typing}
\end{figure*}

As with elaboration (\cref{sec:elaboration}), using \kl{normalised resource
contexts} does not restrict the generality of progress and type preservations
theorems. This is because of the following lemma: if a well-typed \kl{resource
term} is closed, then the context in which it is well-typed must be
\kl{normalised} (\cref{subsec:res_ctx_closed_norm}).

Using these definitions, we can state and prove \kl{progress and type
preservation for resource terms}.

\begin{theorem}[Progress and type preservation for resource terms]
For all closed \kl{resource terms} (\sm{[[ res_term ]]}) which type \kl{check} or
\kl{synthesise} (\sm{[[ cdot ; cdot ; N ; nR |- res_term <= res ]]}) and all
\kl{well-typed heaps} (\sm{[[ h <= nR ]]}) there exists a \kl{resource value}
(\sm{[[ res_val ]]}), \kl(normalised){context} (\sm{[[ nR' ]]}) and \kl{heap}
(\sm{[[ h' ]]}), such that: the \kl(resource){value} is well-typed
(\sm{[[ cdot ; cdot ; N ; nR' |- res_val <= res ]]}); the \kl{heap is well-typed}
(\sm{[[ h' <= nR' ]]}); and, for all frame-\kl{heaps} (\sm{[[ f ]]}), the
\kl{resource term} reduces to the \kl{resource value} without affecting the
frame-\kl{heap} (\sm{[[ _t | < h + f ; res_term > ||v < h' + f ; res_val > ]]}).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
See~\cref{subsec:res_term_progress}, which proceeds by induction on the
\kl{resource term} typing assumption. The \kl(resource){type} dictates the
\kl(resource){value} and \kl(normalised){context}, the latter of which dictates the
shape of the \kl{heap}. This chain relies heavily on a few inversion lemmas.
The existential is necessary later for \fancyref{subsec:wt_values_pat_match}.
The frame heap \sm{f} is necessary later for proving type preservation for
\sm{\cnkw{let}}-expressions.
\end{proof}

\section{Soundness}

The configuration for the operational semantics is a pair of a \kl{heap} and a
\kl{ResCore} program. As one might expect, proving progress requires
\fancyref{subsec:wt_values_pat_match}. \kl{Cerberus} generates all computational
patterns and ensures they are all exhaustive, so I assume this in the proof.
Proving well-typed patterns successfully produce substitutions is complicated
by two things, the solution to which requires the introduction of a relation on
SMT terms and resource types, \sm{[[ res ~ res' ]]}.

The first is that the constraint term generated when typing a computational
pattern (this is required to record, in the constraint context, which branch
the type system is assuming it is in) is not exactly equal to the values it can
match in the operational semantics (nor would we want it to be: the pattern
\sm{[[ Cons ( x1 , x2 ) ]]} should match the value
\sm{[[ Cons ( pval1 , Cons ( pval21 , Nil base_type ( ) ) ) ]]}).
Hence, we must weaken the notion of equality on
types to \sm{~} relatedness, which links the two, so that during the proof,
we can substitute the constraint term \sm{[[ x1 :: x2 ]]} at the type-level, and
maintain a link to the corresponding value.

The second is that the conditions of related \kl{ordered-disjunctions} must
remain SMT-equivalent (with reference to a constraint context), so that
pattern-match typing and \kl{resource term} typing are consistent.

\begin{theorem}[Progress for ResCore]
If a \kl[stmt]{statement} (\sm{[[ stmt ]]}) is well-typed
(\sm{[[ cdot ; cdot ; N ; nR |- stmt <= ret ]]}) and all \kl{computational
patterns} in it are exhaustive, then either it is a \kl(return){value}
(\sm{[[<ret_terms>]]})\sidenote{See~\cref{fig:kernel-expr,fig:typing-ret-terms}.},
or it is unreachable, or for all \kl{well-typed heaps} (\sm{[[ h <= nR ]]}), there
exists another \kl{heap} (\sm{[[ h' ]]}) and \kl[stmt]{statement} (\sm{[[ stmt' ]]})
which is stepped to (\sm{[[ < _t ; h ; stmt > --> < _t ; h' ; stmt' > ]]}) in the
dynamic semantics.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction over the typing assumption. Full proof in~\cref{sec:progress}.
\end{proof}

For type preservation, a few things are noteworthy about its proof.

First is that a frame-\kl{heap} has to be explicitly passed around.  Whilst
this is inconvenient, it becomes necessary in the \textsc{Stmt\_Let} case.

Second is in the lemma \fancyref{subsec:wt_values_pat_match}.
There are two substitutions at play here. As an example, let
the value being matched be \sm{[[ Cons ( Unit , Nil unit ( ) ) , ret_terms ]]} and
the pattern be \sm{[[ comp Cons ( x1 , x2 ) , ret_pat ]]}.

For the return type \sm{[[ sigma y : list unit .: ret ]]}, the type-level
substitution will be \sm{[[ Cons ( x1 , x2 ) / y ( ret )]]}.  However, at the
term-level, the substitution will be \sm{[[ [ Unit / x1 , Nil unit ( ) / x2 ] ]]}.
\emph{Bridging this difference is the main reason for the \sm{[[ ~ ]]} relation}
(the other is the use of \sm{[[ to_fun ret ]]}).  \emph{The type-level substitution
is open}, and only makes sense with respect to the variables introduced in the
pattern-match.

Lastly, we gather constraints throughout the proof, since these are accumulated
by the typing rules, during pattern-matching, case and if. Given
% the constraint context is always well-formed w.r.t.~to the initial logical
% variable context $[[ L0 ]] = [[ allocv : id_map ]]$, this means that after
% substituting in the allocation history,
all the constraints involve no variables,
% hence the substitutions in \textsc{Heap\_Ty} and \textsc{Alloc\_Ty}
they will be trivially decidable.

\begin{theorem}[Type preservation for ResCore]
For all closed and well-typed \kl[stmt]{statements}
(\sm{[[ cdot ; cdot ; N ; nR |- stmt <= ret ]]}),
\kl{well-typed heaps} (\sm{[[ h <= nR ]]}),
frame-\kl{heaps} (\sm{[[ f ]]}),
new \kl{heaps} (\sm{[[ heap ]]}),
and new \kl{statements} (\sm{[[ stmt' ]]}),
which are connected by a step in the dynamic semantics
(\sm{[[ < _t ; h + f ; stmt > -->  < _t ; heap ; stmt' > ]]}),
if all top-level functions are annotated correctly,
there exists a \kl{constraint context} (\sm{[[ N' ]]}),
sub-\kl{heap} (\sm{[[ h' ]]}),
and \kl(normalised){resource context} (\sm{[[ nR' ]]}),
such that the \kl{constraint context} is \sm{[[ N ]]} extended,
the frame is unaffected (\sm{[[ heap ]] = [[ h' + f ]]}),
the sub-\kl{heap is well-typed} (\sm{[[ h' <= nR' ]]}),
and the \kl[stmt]{statement} too
(\sm{[[ cdot ; cdot ; N' ; nR' |- stmt' <= ret ]]}).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction over the typing assumption. Full proof in~\cref{sec:type_preservation}.
\end{proof}
